WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.759
The Little Red Hen

00:00:02.459 --> 00:00:07.536
Once upon a time, on a warm and sunny farmyard, lived a cheerful Little Red Hen.

00:00:07.886 --> 00:00:12.579
She had bright russet-red feathers, lively dark eyes, and a neat yellow beak.

00:00:12.929 --> 00:00:21.249
While the rest of the farm slept, she busily scratched and pecked in her picketty-pecketty way, always looking after her three fluffy yellow chicks.

00:00:21.899 --> 00:00:36.214
Sharing the farmyard were three very lazy friends: a plump pink Pig who loved snoring in the mud sty, a sleek calico Cat curled up asleep on the porch steps, and a chubby grey Rat snoozing by the warm hay bales.

00:00:36.564 --> 00:00:39.209
None of them ever wanted to lift a paw to help.

00:00:39.859 --> 00:00:47.753
One bright morning, while scratching near the wooden garden fence, the Little Red Hen uncovered something unusual in the warm soil.

00:00:48.103 --> 00:00:50.556
It was a slender, gleaming golden seed!

00:00:50.906 --> 00:00:51.930
Cluck, cluck!

00:00:52.280 --> 00:00:53.688
she gasped in wonder.

00:00:54.038 --> 00:00:56.747
It was a seed of wheat, full of promise and life.

00:00:57.397 --> 00:01:04.309
The Little Red Hen knew that if the seed was planted in the rich earth, it would grow into tall wheat to make flour for bread.

00:01:04.659 --> 00:01:09.779
She looked across the farmyard and called out cheerfully: Who will help me plant this wheat seed?

00:01:10.129 --> 00:01:11.004
Not I!

00:01:11.354 --> 00:01:13.338
grunted the Pig from his mud puddle.

00:01:13.688 --> 00:01:14.563
Not I!

00:01:14.913 --> 00:01:16.875
yawned the Cat from the sunny porch.

00:01:17.225 --> 00:01:18.100
Not I!

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.455
squeaked the Rat from behind the hay.

00:01:21.105 --> 00:01:24.028
Then I will plant it myself, said the Little Red Hen.

00:01:24.378 --> 00:01:25.338
And she did!

00:01:25.688 --> 00:01:37.357
With her industrious feet and bill, she carefully dug a neat little furrow in the garden soil, tucked the golden grain inside, smoothed the earth over it, and watered it with care under the morning sun.

00:01:38.007 --> 00:01:42.487
All through the long sunny summer, gentle rains fell and warm breezes blew.

00:01:42.837 --> 00:01:52.587
Day by day, a tiny green shoot poked through the dirt and grew taller and stronger, until a glorious stalk of golden wheat swayed proudly in the autumn breeze.

00:01:53.237 --> 00:01:55.519
At last, the grain was ripe and ready.

00:01:55.869 --> 00:02:01.203
The Little Red Hen looked over at her resting companions and called out: The wheat is ripe!

00:02:01.553 --> 00:02:03.110
Who will help me cut the wheat?

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:04.335
Not I!

00:02:04.685 --> 00:02:07.117
grunted the Pig, rolling onto his side.

00:02:07.467 --> 00:02:08.341
Not I!

00:02:08.691 --> 00:02:11.422
meowed the Cat, lazily stretching her paws.

00:02:11.772 --> 00:02:12.647
Not I!

00:02:12.997 --> 00:02:15.493
squeaked the Rat, twitching his sleepy whiskers.

00:02:16.143 --> 00:02:18.959
Then I will cut it myself, said the Little Red Hen.

00:02:19.309 --> 00:02:20.269
And she did!

00:02:20.619 --> 00:02:31.605
She brought a little hand sickle from the tool shed and carefully reaped the tall golden stalk, laying the rich heads of grain gently upon the ground while her baby chicks chirped with excitement.

00:02:32.255 --> 00:02:37.162
Now the wheat stalks were gathered, but the golden kernels had to be threshed from the chaff.

00:02:37.512 --> 00:02:43.208
Hopeful and patient, the Little Red Hen called across the yard: Who will help me thresh the wheat?

00:02:43.558 --> 00:02:44.433
Not I!

00:02:44.783 --> 00:02:46.681
grunted the Pig with a heavy sigh.

00:02:47.031 --> 00:02:47.906
Not I!

00:02:48.256 --> 00:02:50.304
purred the Cat without opening an eye.

00:02:50.654 --> 00:02:51.529
Not I!

00:02:51.879 --> 00:02:53.905
squeaked the Rat, curling up tighter.

00:02:54.555 --> 00:02:57.649
Then I will thresh it myself, said the Little Red Hen.

00:02:57.999 --> 00:02:58.959
And she did!

00:02:59.309 --> 00:03:10.679
After tucking her three downy chicks into their cozy nest for an afternoon nap, she patiently threshed the stalks until a neat wooden bowl overflowed with clean, plump golden grains.

00:03:11.329 --> 00:03:16.151
The plump grains were gathered, but they needed to be ground into soft white flour.

00:03:16.501 --> 00:03:23.242
The Little Red Hen poured the grain into a sturdy cloth sack and called out: Who will carry this sack of wheat to the mill?

00:03:23.592 --> 00:03:24.467
Not I!

00:03:24.817 --> 00:03:26.950
grunted the Pig, closing his eyes.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.175
Not I!

00:03:28.525 --> 00:03:30.701
meowed the Cat, licking her front paw.

00:03:31.051 --> 00:03:31.925
Not I!

00:03:32.275 --> 00:03:34.366
squeaked the Rat, tucking in his tail.

00:03:35.016 --> 00:03:38.003
Then I will carry it myself, said the Little Red Hen.

00:03:38.353 --> 00:03:39.313
And she did!

00:03:39.663 --> 00:03:46.255
Hoisting the sack over her wing, she trudged down the winding lane past clover fields to the old stone watermill.

00:03:46.605 --> 00:03:54.455
The friendly miller poured the wheat between the turning millstones, and soon she was carrying home a sack of fine, powdery white flour.

00:03:55.105 --> 00:04:03.105
Back home in the cozy farmhouse kitchen, the Little Red Hen tied a fresh white apron around her waist and put on a baker's cap.

00:04:03.455 --> 00:04:06.506
She set out the mixing bowl, yeast, and fresh water.

00:04:06.856 --> 00:04:12.168
Looking out the open kitchen window, she called: Who will help me bake this flour into bread?

00:04:12.518 --> 00:04:13.393
Not I!

00:04:13.743 --> 00:04:16.388
grunted the Pig from the shade of an apple tree.

00:04:16.738 --> 00:04:17.613
Not I!

00:04:17.963 --> 00:04:20.053
chuckled the Cat from her rocking chair.

00:04:20.403 --> 00:04:21.278
Not I!

00:04:21.628 --> 00:04:24.124
snickered the Rat from behind the flour barrel.

00:04:24.774 --> 00:04:27.675
Then I will bake it myself, said the Little Red Hen.

00:04:28.025 --> 00:04:28.985
And she did!

00:04:29.335 --> 00:04:38.317
She mixed the flour and water, kneaded the smooth dough with gentle care, shaped it into round loaves, and slipped them into the warm brick hearth oven.

00:04:38.667 --> 00:04:46.709
Before long, the most delicious, mouth-watering aroma of fresh baking bread drifted out the window and across the whole farmyard!

00:04:47.359 --> 00:04:54.911
At last, the oven door opened, and out came two golden-brown loaves of bread, crusty and steaming and smelling wonderful.

00:04:55.261 --> 00:05:03.069
Instantly, the Pig, the Cat, and the Rat scrambled to the kitchen window, their eyes wide and their mouths watering hungrily.

00:05:03.419 --> 00:05:08.134
The Little Red Hen looked at them through the window and asked: Who will help me eat the bread?

00:05:08.484 --> 00:05:09.316
I will!

00:05:09.666 --> 00:05:11.735
shouted the Pig, jumping to his feet.

00:05:12.085 --> 00:05:12.917
I will!

00:05:13.267 --> 00:05:15.379
cried the Cat, leaping onto the sill.

00:05:15.729 --> 00:05:16.561
I will!

00:05:16.911 --> 00:05:19.002
squeaked the Rat, hopping up and down.

00:05:19.352 --> 00:05:20.611
Oh no, you won't!

00:05:20.961 --> 00:05:23.606
smiled the Little Red Hen with a cheerful cluck.

00:05:23.956 --> 00:05:30.185
I planted the seed, I cut the wheat, I threshed the grain, I carried it to the mill, and I baked the bread.

00:05:30.535 --> 00:05:33.565
My three little chicks and I will eat it all ourselves!

00:05:33.915 --> 00:05:41.211
And she sliced the warm crusty loaf, spread it with sweet butter, and shared every delicious bite with her happy little chicks.

00:05:41.561 --> 00:05:42.521
And they did!
